  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 156 PM CDT Fri Oct 16 2026\n\nWith the advection of cooler air in the wake of yesterdays cold \nfront, temperatures throughout the rest of the day today will be in \nthe upper 40s and low 50s. Synoptically, a 500mb positively tilted \ntrough is digging over our region, with strong winds aloft as the \njet stream passes right overhead. This has allowed for a large \npressure gradient in response to high pressure over the western \nGreat Plains, and the low pressure system that exited to our east \nyesterday. As a result, expect gusty winds out of the north up to \n30mph, before diminishing in the evening hours as we lose the \ndaytime mixing. Throughout the evening and overnight hours, the \nsurface high to our west, along with dry air to our north will both \napproach our area. This will help push off any remaining cloud \ncover, and allow temperatures to drop into the 30s. Portions of \nareas north of Interstate 70 will likely dip down into the low 30s, \nprompting the issuance of a Freeze Warning to our northern counties. \nThe rest of the area will get down into the mid-30s, with many of \nthose areas having a Frost Advisory in effect. Precaution will \ninclude protecting any sensitive plants, crops, and vegetation.\n\nSaturday and Sunday will be quiet as our trough moves off to the \neast, along with the high pressure approaching. We will also see \nlittle if any cloud cover this weekend, as drier air advects from \nour north. Temperatures will also climb back up into the 60s, \nespecially on Sunday as pressure heights increase and warm air \nadvection moves into our region. Conditions will remain quiet \nthroughout next week, with the ridge staying in place, and allowing \nsoutherly flow to advect warm air. Temperatures then will return \ninto the 70s and 80s by the middle part of the week. While this \nhappens, a trough will begin to develop off the west coast, and will \nstrengthen as it interacts with a subtropical jet. As this \ndisturbance gets its act together, it will move into our area by \nlate next week and into the weekend. Exact timing and effects still \nremain to be seen, as certainty will increase as we get closer to \nthe time period.",
